course: language
lesson: 9
title: Irregular verbs: goed, brung and other brave mistakes
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: Most English verbs make the past by adding -ed: jump, jumped; these are regular verbs, but about 200 common ones break the rule: go, went; run, ran; catch, caught; these are irregular.
explain: When little Ava says "I goed to the park and runned", she isn't being silly: she has discovered the -ed rule all by herself and uses it everywhere; she'll learn "went" and "ran" one by one by hearing them.
explain: Irregular verbs come in little families: sing, sang, sung; swim, swam, swum; ring, rang, rung; and bring, brought; think, thought; buy, bought; catch, caught; teach, taught.
explain: Some forms are patchwork from history: "went" was once the past of an old verb "wend" (to go), and English borrowed it for go hundreds of years ago.
explain: Verbs change over time and between countries: Americans often say dove and snuck where British say dived and sneaked; and many dialects say "I seen him at the chippy", which is dialect, not stupidity.
fact: A regular verb adds -ed for the past (jump, jumped); an irregular verb changes in another way (go, went).
fact: English has about 200 common irregular verbs, and children learn them one by one.
fact: Saying "goed" shows a child has found the -ed rule; the kind response is to say "went" back naturally.
fact: Bring goes bring, brought, brought, like think, thought and buy, bought; "brung" is heard in some dialects, especially in the US, but is not standard English.
fact: Many irregular verbs have become regular over hundreds of years; reach once had an old past form like "raught" and now just says reached.
fact: In American English "dove" is common for the past of dive; British English says "dived"; both are correct in their own country.
fact: Swim goes swim, swam, swum: "I swam" alone and "I have swum" after have; sing, sang, sung and ring, rang, rung work the same way.
fact: "I seen" is common in many British, Irish and American dialects; standard English says "I saw" or "I have seen". A chippy is a fish and chip shop.
fact: "Went" was once the past of the old verb "wend", meaning to go; when a verb's forms come from different words, it is called suppletion, and "be" (am, is, was, were) is built the same patchwork way.
fact: Catch goes catch, caught, caught, which rhymes with teach, taught; "sus" is slang for suspicious or a bit dodgy, made hugely popular with kids partly by the game Among Us.
fact: "Snuck" began in American dialects and is now very common in the US; in British English "sneaked" is the standard written form, and both are in major dictionaries.
fact: Drink goes drink, drank, drunk: "I drank" and "I have drunk"; people sometimes say "I've drank" to dodge the other meaning of drunk, but "I have drunk" is correct.
fact: Fly goes fly, flew, flown; in baseball "he flied out" (hit a high ball that was caught) is regular because it comes from the noun "fly ball".
exercise: Little Ava says "I goed to the park." What does this show? | She has learned the -ed rule and is using it everywhere | She is not learning properly and needs extra lessons | She is making fun of her brother | She heard "goed" from grown-ups
exercise: What is an irregular verb? | One that doesn't make its past with -ed | One that is only used sometimes, not every day | One that has no past at all | One that only appears in questions
exercise: Tyler says "I brung my lunch." What is the standard past of bring? | brought | brang | bringed | brung
exercise: Which verb follows the same pattern as bring, brought? | think, thought | sing, sang | ring, rang | swim, swam
exercise: Max asks why reach isn't "raught". What is true? | It once had a form like that but became regular | Reach has always been irregular, so raught is the real form | Reach has always been irregular | Raught is used in Scotland only
exercise: An American friend says "I dove into the pool." What would most British people say? | I dived into the pool | I diven into the pool | I dove into the pool too | I doved into the pool
exercise: Rehan says "I swimmed across the pool." What should it be? | I swam across the pool | I swum across the pool | I swimmed across the pool | I have swam across the pool
exercise: Which is correct? | I have swum two lengths | I have swam two lengths | I have swimmed two lengths | I swum two lengths
exercise: Grandad from Lancashire says "I seen him at the chippy." What is a chippy? | A fish and chip shop | A carpenter's shop | A computer shop | A baby bird
exercise: In standard written English, Grandad's sentence is... | I saw him at the chippy | I seed him at the chippy | I have saw him at the chippy | I sawed him at the chippy
exercise: Why does "went" look nothing like "go"? | It was once the past of an old verb, wend | It is just "go" spelled backwards and changed a bit | It was a printing mistake | It comes from French
exercise: Kai writes "I catched the ball". What's the past of catch? | caught | catched | cought | cotch
exercise: At a British spelling bee the word is the past of sneak. What's the standard British form? | sneaked | snuck | snook | sneaken
exercise: Dad says "I've drank all the milk." What is the careful form? | I have drunk all the milk | I have drinked all the milk | I have drank all the milk | I have drunken all the milk
exercise: What's the past of fly in "The baby bird ___ away"? | flew | flied | flown | flyed
exercise: Why do baseball players say "he flied out"? | It comes from the noun "fly ball", so it's regular | It is a mistake that every baseball player makes | Baseball has its own grammar law | Birds in baseball fly differently
exercise: Which list has only irregular verbs? | swim, catch, think | jump, walk, play | swim, jump, think | catch, walk, teach
exercise: Sing, sang, sung. If a made-up verb "zing" followed the same family, what would "I have ___" be? | zung | zang | zinged | zingt
test: Your mate laughs at his little sister for "I runned fast." What's the kind thing to tell him? | She's found the -ed rule; just say "ran" back to her | She'll never learn English properly if she talks like that | She should be told off | "Runned" is the correct old form
test: An American kid says "I snuck a cookie, no cap." ("No cap" means no lie.) Is "snuck" a real word? | Yes, it's very common in American English | No, it's just slang and you won't find it in dictionaries | No, it's only used in Scotland | Yes, but only for cookies
test: Your gran from Lancashire says "Who's seen me glasses? I seen 'em on the side." Should you correct her? | No need; she's speaking her dialect, and you write "saw" | Yes, loudly, so she learns to stop using dialect words | Yes, dialects are mistakes | No, because "seen" is the only correct form
test: Tyler argues "sing, sang, sung, so bring, brang, brung, innit." What's wrong with his logic? | The pattern is real, but bring belongs to the -ought family | Nothing, because brung is standard English in every country | Sing is irregular but bring is regular | Sing should be sing, singed, singed
test: What would happen if every verb became regular? | We'd say "goed" and "thinked", and the old forms would vanish | Nothing at all, because verbs never really change their forms | English would lose its past tense | Every verb would end in -ought
test: A teacher writes "Your story is lowkey sus: 'I teached my dog a trick.'" ("Lowkey" means a bit.) What's the fix? | I taught my dog a trick | I teached my dog a trick | I tought my dog a trick | I have teached my dog a trick
test: In a cartoon, a caveman says "Me catched fish, me bringed it home." Fix the verbs only. | caught, brought | catched, brang | cotch, brung | caught, brang
test: Your cousin thinks irregular verbs never change. What fact proves her wrong? | Many irregular verbs became regular over time, like reach | Irregular verbs are fixed by law and can never be changed | All irregular verbs are new | No irregular verb has ever changed
test: A British teacher marks "dove" wrong in an American boy's story. Is that fair? | It's correct in American English, so it's a country difference | Yes, because dove is only a bird, never a verb in English | Yes, it's a spelling mistake | No, dove is the only correct form everywhere
test: Dad, embarrassed, says "I can't say 'I've drunk the milk', sounds dodgy." What's true? | "I have drunk" is correct; the funny double meaning doesn't change that | "I've drank" is the correct form, because drunk means something else | "I've drinked" is the polite form | You can never say you drank milk
test: Which past form is a wrong "-ed" guess, like goed? | thinked | jumped | played | watched
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises an irregular verb? | A verb whose past forms are not made by adding "-ed" | A verb employed only in formal registers and official writing | A verb lacking any past form | A verb confined to interrogative sentences
exam: Identify the principal reason for which a young child may produce the form "goed". | The child has inferred the general rule and applied it to an exception | The child is incapable of acquiring language | The child has copied the form from adult speech | The form "goed" is standard in contemporary English
exam: Ascertain the form consistent with standard English for the sentence "I have ___ two lengths of the pool" (verb: swim). | swum | swam | swimmed | swimming
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the past forms "dived" and "dove". | The former is standard in British usage and the latter common in American usage | The latter is erroneous in every variety of English | The former is erroneous in every variety of English | Neither form exists in any dictionary
